# Deliberately narrow. There is NO global `* text=auto` rule here: the only
# CRLF file in the tracked tree is retired mcp/search_symbols.py, and a global
# rule would renormalise it (and anything else a teammate is mid-edit on) into
# a diff nobody asked for. Add rules here one file type at a time, with a
# reason.

# Board files are checked in as text, not binary. `text eol=lf` normalises them
# to LF in the repo and on checkout on every platform; `-text` would be the
# other obvious choice and is WRONG here, because git would then render every
# board change as "binary files differ". Reviewing a placement change means
# diffing the s-expressions -- seeing which (at x y rot) moved and by how much
# -- and that is the whole reason the board file is the API. The one tracked
# board, engine/tests/fixtures/ref.kicad_pcb, is already pure LF, so this rule
# pins the status quo rather than rewriting anything.
*.kicad_pcb text eol=lf
*.kicad_sch text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF whatever machine committed them: a CRLF shebang line
# makes the kernel look for an interpreter named "/bin/sh\r" and the script
# dies with a bare "not found" that names no cause. The team pushes from mixed
# Windows/macOS machines and CI runs these on Linux.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Vendored reference code is read-only (see CLAUDE.md): never normalise it.
vendor/** -text
